m WritableStream \u2192 called with cls=Sub \u2192 line 30 False \u2192 line 32 returns NotImplemented.\n\nGreat, so tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom pysnooper.utils import WritableStream\n\n\ndef test_abstract_write_body_executes():\n    \"\"\"Calling WritableStream.write directly executes the abstract method body.\"\"\"\n    class Dummy:\n        pass\n    result = WritableStream.write(Dummy(), 'hello')\n    assert result is None\n\n\ndef test_subclasshook_positive():\n    class HasWrite:\n        def write(self, s):\n            return s\n    assert issubclass(HasWrite, WritableStream) is True\n\n\ndef test_subclasshook_negative_missing_method():\n    class NoWrite:\n        pass\n    assert issubclass(NoWrite, WritableStream) is False\n\n\ndef test_subclasshook_not_implemented_branch():\n    class Concrete(WritableStream):\n        def write(self, s):\n            return s\n    # cls is Concrete, not WritableStream -> NotImplemented branch\n    assert issubclass(Concrete, Concrete) is True  # fallback normal behaviour\n    assert issubclass(int, Concrete) is False\n```\n\nWait, for `test_subclasshook_not_implemented_branch`: `issubclass(Concrete, Concrete)` \u2014 check_class(Concrete, Concrete): looks up `__subclasshook__` on Concrete \u2192 found in WritableStream via MRO \u2192 called with cls=Concrete \u2192 line 30: `cls is WritableStream` \u2192 False \u2192 line 32: return NotImplemented \u2192 fallback to normal issubclass \u2192 Concrete is a subclass of itself \u2192 True. Covers line 32. \n\nBut hmm \u2014 is there a risk that `issubclass(Concrete, Concrete)` short-circuits before calling the hook? Like `if derived is cls: return 1`? Let me check CPython's recursive_isinstance/issubclass logic:\n\n```c\nint\nPyObject_IsSubclass(PyObject *derived, PyObject *cls)\n{\n    ...\n    if (PyTuple_Check(cls)) {...}\n\n    if (PyType_Check(cls)) {\n        retval = check_class(cls, derived);\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nHmm, is there a fast path `derive